# SRG-APP-000251-WSR-000195  -  CAT II
# The web server must terminate the connection if server-level exceptions
# are triggered when handling requests to prevent HTTP request smuggling
# attacks.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000251-WSR-000195
group:           V-264365
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   UNDETERMINED
grade:           E
decidable:       manual
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-001310
control_rev5:    SI-10

contribution:    Nothing configurable: whether the splunkd HTTP server and the Python
                 application server tear the socket down rather than return it to the
                 keep-alive pool when a server-level exception is raised is internal,
                 non-configurable product behavior, so the application has no setting
                 bearing on this requirement.

external:        The bundled CherryPy-based application server and the splunkd HTTP server
                 themselves: the teardown behavior is fixed in the product build, so any
                 deficiency is a vendor matter recorded against the product rather than
                 against the configuration baseline.
external:        A behavioral test procedure — a request that forces a server-side
                 exception against each listener in scope (management and REST port, Splunk
                 Web, HTTP Event Collector) with the exchange captured — is required to
                 decide the requirement, and is carried by the assessment procedure, not by
                 configuration review.
external:        Termination of connections on back-end exceptions where a reverse proxy or
                 load balancer fronts Splunk, carried by that component and assessed
                 against its own baseline.

manual_reason:   The requirement concerns whether the HTTP server tears the connection down
                 when an exception is raised during request handling, not whether the
                 exception is disclosed, which is a separate requirement. Splunk Enterprise
                 exposes no setting that conditions a connection close on a server-level
                 exception: the whole server.conf [httpServer] stanza
                 (server.conf.spec:1291 onward, 26 keys) carries no exception-handling or
                 connection-termination key, and the nearest web.conf [settings] keys
                 govern disclosure (request.show_tracebacks, web.conf.spec:910),
                 application server shutdown (appServerProcessShutdownTimeout,
                 web.conf.spec:1278) and response timeouts (response.timeout,
                 web.conf.spec:971) — none of which closes a connection because an
                 exception occurred. checks.tsv accordingly attaches no key to this
                 requirement. Absence of a setting is not absence of the behavior, so the
                 requirement is settled by sending a request that forces a server-side
                 exception to each listener in scope and observing whether the response
                 carries Connection: close and the TCP connection is torn down rather than
                 reused; configuration cannot decide it in either direction.

citation:        SRG-APP-000251-WSR-000195 check and fix text (CCI-001310, SI-10); no
                 product STIG rule shares CCI-001310; no conf, stanza or key is attached to
                 this requirement in the mechanism inventory. Splunk 10.x specification set
                 (Version 10.0.0), keys enumerated and excluded as non-implementing:
                 server.conf.spec:1291 stanza [httpServer] in full; web.conf.spec:910
                 request.show_tracebacks; web.conf.spec:971 response.timeout;
                 web.conf.spec:1278 appServerProcessShutdownTimeout.
finding:         No Splunk Enterprise setting governs connection termination when a
                 server-level exception is raised, so this requirement is not decided by
                 application configuration: any finding rests on the listener's observed
                 teardown behavior, which is fixed in the product build, or on the
                 front-end proxy that terminates the connection, and is recorded against
                 those rather than against the Splunk application configuration assessed
                 here.
